{
  "_comment": [
    "A core rocket with TWO strap-on boosters, flown by both the engine we ship and",
    "pristine upstream core.",
    "",
    "Different shape of problem from the two serial fixtures. The boosters are a",
    "ParallelStage hanging off the core's body tube, not a stage stacked underneath,",
    "so the thing under test is radial placement: instanceCount, radiusMethod and",
    "radiusOffset (a GAP from the parent's surface, not a radius from the",
    "centerline), angleMethod and angleOffset. A ParallelStage is still an",
    "AxialStage, so it separates at booster burnout and flies its own branch, which",
    "is what makes this comparable to the serial cases at all.",
    "",
    "Mirrors ParityMain.podScenarios case C, with one deliberate difference: that",
    "scenario caps maxTime at 6 s and this does not. The cap is there because parity",
    "compares whole recorded series, and the tail of a chuteless tumble drifts the",
    "sample count by one. This comparison reads event sequences, apogee and",
    "separation time, none of which the drift touches, and the full flight was",
    "checked to agree - so the fixture covers the core's apogee, chute deployment and",
    "ground hit rather than stopping before any of them.",
    "",
    "The leading SIM_WARN on the core branch is expected and appears on both sides:",
    "the booster tubes have no nose cone, so the airframe is open at the front.",
    "",
    "Same rules as the other fixtures: every aero- and mass-relevant property",
    "explicit, and every component that can be an event source named. See",
    "staged-flight.json for why both of those matter."
  ],
